      ],
      "build": {
        "segment": "_TEXT",
        "public": "_rle_packbits_decode",
        "span": 4,
        "flags_append": "",
        "bindings": {},
        "module_segments": {}
      },
      "evidence": "F_6D86 starts at 0x6F86, word-aligned with the PAD_006F85 pad TLINK supplies before a word-aligned 'segment word public CODE' module. F_6D86 ends at 0x6DC5 (module-relative); the seven following bytes (PAD_006FC5) are the LZ stage's CS-addressed bit-reader state, not alignment padding, and F_6DCC begins at 0x6DCC. F_6EFF (0x70FF) and F_6F4B (0x714B) both start at ODD addresses immediately following the routine before them in this same module, so neither can be the head of its own word-aligned module: each was assembled directly behind its predecessor in one TASM module. One fresh TASM object (asm/DECODE.ASM, extending the previously-proven asm/M_6D86_6DCC.ASM with F_6EFF and F_6F4B appended) reproduces the full byte range and all four public offsets identically to the original per-member bytes (probe_tu.py's member-offset check reports a spurious 'public None' mismatch for the PAD_006FC5 member -- a pre-existing tool limitation confirmed identical against the unmodified asm/M_6D86_6DCC.ASM baseline -- but the reconstructed and original hex dumps are byte-for-byte identical; python tools/probe_module.py M_6D86_6F4B reports EXACT, 573 bytes, 19 fixups, matching the members' standalone sum of 19+0+0)."
    },
